WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:12.330 --> 00:00:13.413
- Welcome everyone.

00:00:14.306 --> 00:00:18.120
I'm Saad Omer and my title
for the next six weeks is the

00:00:18.120 --> 00:00:20.570
Director of the Yale
Institute for Global Health.

00:00:21.660 --> 00:00:26.220
And I'm also a professor both
in the schools of medicine

00:00:26.220 --> 00:00:28.383
and public health here at Yale.

00:00:29.459 --> 00:00:31.530
As most of you would know,

00:00:31.530 --> 00:00:36.090
I am delighted to welcome
you to our last conversation

00:00:36.090 --> 00:00:41.090
series event for this academic year.

00:00:41.190 --> 00:00:45.480
As you know, this is
the Conversation series,

00:00:45.480 --> 00:00:47.610
global Health Conversation
Series is run by the Yale

00:00:47.610 --> 00:00:49.350
Institute for Global Health,

00:00:49.350 --> 00:00:52.420
but this event is in
collaboration with the Yale

00:00:53.729 --> 00:00:56.400
School of Public Health.

00:00:56.400 --> 00:00:58.611
Specifically with the

00:00:58.611 --> 00:01:03.611
epidemiology of Microbial
Diseases seminar series as well.

00:01:04.050 --> 00:01:04.883
The overall,

00:01:05.803 --> 00:01:08.400
the conversation series is all
co-sponsored by the McMillan

00:01:08.400 --> 00:01:11.523
Center and their Walker fund.

00:01:12.480 --> 00:01:16.590
As we have learned through the
COVID 19 pandemic that health

00:01:16.590 --> 00:01:19.140
transcends boundaries.

00:01:19.140 --> 00:01:20.199
You know,

00:01:20.199 --> 00:01:22.890
something that emerges in
China, shows up in Washington,

00:01:22.890 --> 00:01:27.060
and then within a week and
a half in Connecticut and,

00:01:27.060 --> 00:01:31.110
and in many parts of the
country and the world.

00:01:31.110 --> 00:01:32.460
We have also learned that by,

00:01:32.460 --> 00:01:34.983
by promoting healthy behaviors,

00:01:35.850 --> 00:01:39.270
investing in healthcare
infrastructure and specifically with

00:01:39.270 --> 00:01:44.270
a focus on equity helps everyone,

00:01:44.340 --> 00:01:46.743
both domestically and internationally,

00:01:47.616 --> 00:01:51.630
especially if these lessons are shared.

00:01:51.630 --> 00:01:55.020
And the beauty of this country
is cooperative federalism

00:01:55.020 --> 00:02:00.020
kind of infrastructure, is
that we have at least 50 labs.

00:02:00.570 --> 00:02:05.250
Unfortunately, not all those
lab experiments go well, but,

00:02:05.250 --> 00:02:07.590
but the idea is that we
learn from each other,

00:02:07.590 --> 00:02:12.100
especially if things are
implemented in good faith with the

00:02:13.410 --> 00:02:18.240
best possible interpretation of evidence,

00:02:18.240 --> 00:02:20.940
but also evaluated and shared

00:02:20.940 --> 00:02:25.940
so that's the beauty of
treating the local health as

00:02:28.920 --> 00:02:33.920
part of a global whole
that we all benefit from.

00:02:36.660 --> 00:02:38.700
So in today's event,

00:02:38.700 --> 00:02:41.550
we will discuss these important
issues and how global health

00:02:41.550 --> 00:02:46.550
is essentially sort of derives
a lot of its strength from

00:02:47.400 --> 00:02:49.320
for what happens locally.

00:02:49.320 --> 00:02:50.310
And for our discussion,

00:02:50.310 --> 00:02:52.860
it is my immense pleasure to,

00:02:52.860 --> 00:02:56.160
to welcome two distinguished
experts and leaders

00:02:56.160 --> 00:02:58.280
joining us today.

00:02:58.280 --> 00:03:00.930
So the first one is Dr. Manisha Juthani.

00:03:00.930 --> 00:03:02.040
She's one of our own,

00:03:02.040 --> 00:03:07.040
she's on public service leave
from the School of Medicine

00:03:08.310 --> 00:03:10.310
and where she's a professor of medicine,

00:03:11.310 --> 00:03:14.130
her day job these days is
the commissioner of the

00:03:14.130 --> 00:03:16.781
Connecticut Department of Public Health,

00:03:16.781 --> 00:03:20.880
a department which was actually
owes a lot of its existence

00:03:20.880 --> 00:03:23.770
and founding to Dr. Winslow,

00:03:23.770 --> 00:03:27.431
who after whom this auditorium is named.

00:03:27.431 --> 00:03:29.877
And so this institution has a,

00:03:29.877 --> 00:03:33.510
a long history of contributing
to the state's public health

00:03:33.510 --> 00:03:35.640
infrastructure and,

00:03:35.640 --> 00:03:38.040
and she's the first Indian
American to serve as a

00:03:38.040 --> 00:03:41.010
commissioner in the state of Connecticut.

00:03:41.010 --> 00:03:42.750
So again,

00:03:42.750 --> 00:03:47.750
is a leader in from that aspect
as well as we emerge from

00:03:48.030 --> 00:03:48.863
the pandemic.

00:03:48.863 --> 00:03:51.900
She obviously helped
lead the covid response,

00:03:51.900 --> 00:03:55.250
but she continues to focus and,

00:03:55.250 --> 00:03:58.260
and that comes into
relief a little bit more,

00:03:58.260 --> 00:04:02.310
efforts to deal with the
other public health issues,

00:04:02.310 --> 00:04:07.020
including the tragic opioid
crisis, lead abatement,

00:04:07.020 --> 00:04:08.190
gun violence,

00:04:08.190 --> 00:04:11.670
and especially evidence-based
approaches to addressing it,

00:04:11.670 --> 00:04:15.080
mental health, climate and health.

00:04:15.080 --> 00:04:18.420
So that's an existential
threat we have that transcends

00:04:18.420 --> 00:04:22.050
boundaries, has global
and local implications.

00:04:22.050 --> 00:04:23.917
Maternal health and,

00:04:23.917 --> 00:04:26.460
and drinking water infrastructure
improvements are some of

00:04:26.460 --> 00:04:28.590
the other areas that she's focusing on.

00:04:28.590 --> 00:04:31.680
She's committed to growing the
public health workforce and

00:04:31.680 --> 00:04:34.607
which y'all are part of the public health,

00:04:34.607 --> 00:04:36.257
emerging public health workforce.

00:04:37.251 --> 00:04:42.210
And someone told me there are
40% vacancies are open in the

00:04:42.210 --> 00:04:44.850
department of public health.

00:04:44.850 --> 00:04:46.560
Is that a?

00:04:46.560 --> 00:04:48.900
- Little bit less than that,
but yeah, we're hiring.

00:04:48.900 --> 00:04:51.210
- So exactly, that's the point.

00:04:51.210 --> 00:04:55.383
And especially those of you
who are sort of, you know,

00:04:55.383 --> 00:04:59.790
formatting your CVs for May and
the ones who are graduating,

00:04:59.790 --> 00:05:03.180
one of the best things to do
right after an MPH program is

00:05:03.180 --> 00:05:07.590
to work for a good well run
state health department,

00:05:07.590 --> 00:05:09.953
and especially if you don't
have to break your lease.

00:05:10.950 --> 00:05:12.900
And so, you know, that's a,

00:05:12.900 --> 00:05:14.940
that's a pitch of staying
engaged, et cetera,

00:05:14.940 --> 00:05:16.830
and including those who want to have a,

00:05:16.830 --> 00:05:21.180
a subsequent research career
as well and research or a

00:05:21.180 --> 00:05:22.013
career in medicine.

00:05:22.013 --> 00:05:23.640
So this is,

00:05:23.640 --> 00:05:26.310
so not just those who wants to, you know,

00:05:26.310 --> 00:05:29.943
work in a health department
for the long term as well.

00:05:30.840 --> 00:05:34.440
My other guest is Dr. Umair Shah.

00:05:34.440 --> 00:05:37.800
He's the Secretary of Health
for the State of Washington.

00:05:37.800 --> 00:05:41.760
And he made that trip from Texas.

00:05:41.760 --> 00:05:45.568
So his training was at Vanderbilt and,

00:05:45.568 --> 00:05:48.060
and a few other places and then was the,

00:05:48.060 --> 00:05:51.900
the health commissioner for
Harris County around Houston and

00:05:51.900 --> 00:05:56.900
led the response both in
emergencies and non-emergency sort

00:05:57.780 --> 00:06:01.710
of healthcare functions for
that important populous and

00:06:01.710 --> 00:06:06.510
diverse county in the US and
was there when, you know,

00:06:06.510 --> 00:06:08.037
around Katrina and,

00:06:08.037 --> 00:06:13.037
and some other major disasters
around Ebola and Zika and

00:06:14.041 --> 00:06:17.980
sort of all sorts of
things that came his way.

00:06:19.811 --> 00:06:22.950
It was almost like a sandbox
video game where, you know,

00:06:22.950 --> 00:06:26.458
things were, you know,
thrown in his direction.

00:06:26.458 --> 00:06:28.890
and he, you know,

00:06:28.890 --> 00:06:32.815
came out of that stronger and
with several lessons that he

00:06:32.815 --> 00:06:35.220
took with him to the state of Washington,

00:06:35.220 --> 00:06:36.053
but beyond as well.

00:06:36.053 --> 00:06:39.600
He's very active in the
National Academies of medicine.

00:06:39.600 --> 00:06:43.050
So over his career he has been
a clinician and innovator,

00:06:43.050 --> 00:06:46.440
an educator and a leader in health.

00:06:46.440 --> 00:06:50.910
He has been particularly
a champion of underserved

00:06:50.910 --> 00:06:55.170
communities, but with a
focus of substantive action,

00:06:55.170 --> 00:06:58.830
not just sort of talking
about it, which is important,

00:06:58.830 --> 00:07:00.450
but we're taking action.

00:07:00.450 --> 00:07:02.640
and he's,

00:07:02.640 --> 00:07:05.850
his career has always been at
the intersection of health and

00:07:05.850 --> 00:07:08.023
healthcare while charting a,

00:07:08.023 --> 00:07:12.960
a fresh course in health by
centering cornerstone values of

00:07:12.960 --> 00:07:15.342
equity, innovation and engagement.

00:07:15.342 --> 00:07:19.500
So we are honored to have you
both here and I'll start us

00:07:19.500 --> 00:07:21.780
off with a few questions for,

00:07:21.780 --> 00:07:24.330
for the two of you and
then we'll open it up

00:07:24.330 --> 00:07:26.177
to the audience.

00:07:26.177 --> 00:07:27.570
So the first question
is actually both of you

00:07:30.180 --> 00:07:33.783
and both of you are, you know, to,

00:07:35.642 --> 00:07:37.170
to use a military term war tested,

00:07:37.170 --> 00:07:40.173
combat tested generals in public health.

00:07:41.520 --> 00:07:45.630
What is the immediate course
of action to be taken locally

00:07:45.630 --> 00:07:47.580
when a global health event occurs?

00:07:47.580 --> 00:07:49.980
So maybe Dr. Shah, I can
start with you and then

00:07:49.980 --> 00:07:50.913
with Dr. Juthani.

00:07:53.280 --> 00:07:55.020
- Well first of all, thank you Dr. Omer

00:07:55.020 --> 00:07:59.850
for having us as part of
this convening and thank you

00:07:59.850 --> 00:08:01.410
interim Dean Pedigrew and,

00:08:01.410 --> 00:08:03.840
and the entire school of
public health and just really

00:08:03.840 --> 00:08:06.060
appreciative of the
opportunity to be here.

00:08:06.060 --> 00:08:08.640
So, you know,

00:08:08.640 --> 00:08:12.993
I think this is really
the defining question.

00:08:14.130 --> 00:08:16.410
As we are coming out of a pandemic,

00:08:16.410 --> 00:08:20.610
we see the question for the
future about when something

00:08:20.610 --> 00:08:25.020
happens globally, how do we act locally?

00:08:25.020 --> 00:08:27.870
And I think this really
underscores the intersection of

00:08:27.870 --> 00:08:32.870
global and domestic health that
we no longer can be thinking

00:08:32.970 --> 00:08:37.710
about watching something on
the news cycle and recognizing

00:08:37.710 --> 00:08:41.880
that it's over there 'cause
there is no over there,

00:08:41.880 --> 00:08:44.010
over there is here.

00:08:44.010 --> 00:08:47.700
And very quickly something
that can happen in Hartford or

00:08:47.700 --> 00:08:52.700
New Haven can spread all the
way across the globe and flip

00:08:53.100 --> 00:08:56.190
it around the bidirectionality
of the fact that it can

00:08:56.190 --> 00:08:57.840
happen elsewhere.

00:08:57.840 --> 00:09:00.090
And now we are dealing with it here.

00:09:00.090 --> 00:09:05.090
I always saw with Covid
19 initially when, and,

00:09:05.097 --> 00:09:07.653
and obviously, you know, as Dr. Omer said,

00:09:07.653 --> 00:09:09.570
I was in Houston, I was in Texas,

00:09:09.570 --> 00:09:13.740
but the state of Washington had
the first case, as you know,

00:09:13.740 --> 00:09:17.849
in the continental United
States of Covid 19.

00:09:17.849 --> 00:09:18.682
We had the first case,

00:09:18.682 --> 00:09:21.660
we had the first
unfortunately first outbreak,

00:09:21.660 --> 00:09:24.120
and we also had the first death.

00:09:24.120 --> 00:09:27.030
These are milestones,

00:09:27.030 --> 00:09:30.480
they were unfortunate and sad milestones,

00:09:30.480 --> 00:09:33.090
but they were also important
for the rest of the nation

00:09:33.090 --> 00:09:37.620
because what was happening in
Washington, people like us,

00:09:37.620 --> 00:09:39.960
whether it was Connecticut or in Texas,

00:09:39.960 --> 00:09:44.040
were watching it play out and
what was happening in one part

00:09:44.040 --> 00:09:46.500
of the nation or one part of
the globe as we were learning

00:09:46.500 --> 00:09:48.900
of what was happening
in obviously in China.

00:09:48.900 --> 00:09:51.570
But we were also seeing it
spreading through Iran and

00:09:51.570 --> 00:09:55.290
spreading through Europe and
spreading all the way across to

00:09:55.290 --> 00:09:59.430
other nation states were
important lessons for all of us.

00:09:59.430 --> 00:10:04.110
And so I'm very used to, in
Houston, hurricane lines,

00:10:04.110 --> 00:10:06.000
you all the spaghetti
lines you see, you know,

00:10:06.000 --> 00:10:08.460
there's a storm that's about
to hit and you see all these

00:10:08.460 --> 00:10:11.820
lines and I remember very
early in the pandemic thinking

00:10:11.820 --> 00:10:16.820
about where is the actual
spaghetti line for Covid 19.

00:10:16.830 --> 00:10:19.500
The first thing that all of us
in public health were trying

00:10:19.500 --> 00:10:23.340
to do was to figure out where do we get,

00:10:23.340 --> 00:10:26.970
where are we going to have
our first case in our local

00:10:26.970 --> 00:10:28.867
jurisdiction?

00:10:28.867 --> 00:10:30.480
And that was, it was,

00:10:30.480 --> 00:10:34.050
it was really a disease
detective case of trying to find

00:10:34.050 --> 00:10:35.370
that first case.

00:10:35.370 --> 00:10:40.370
And ultimately you had the
spaghetti lines of hurricanes and

00:10:41.430 --> 00:10:44.850
storms that were pointing to
where was gonna happen across

00:10:44.850 --> 00:10:47.100
the country and it just
happened to be just north of

00:10:47.100 --> 00:10:47.933
Seattle.

00:10:48.921 --> 00:10:50.321
So I think the key question,

00:10:51.473 --> 00:10:54.420
the real response is that we
cannot sit and rest on our

00:10:54.420 --> 00:10:56.700
laurels when something is
happening elsewhere because

00:10:56.700 --> 00:10:58.683
ultimately it will impact us locally.

00:11:02.040 --> 00:11:04.410
- Thank you for the introduction.

00:11:04.410 --> 00:11:09.410
So great to be here with
you Umair and all the Yale

00:11:09.930 --> 00:11:13.110
community that I've been
a part of for a long time.

00:11:13.110 --> 00:11:16.110
You know, one, I wanna make one
other plug for the students,

00:11:16.110 --> 00:11:19.380
which is there is also a
Connecticut Department of Public

00:11:19.380 --> 00:11:21.840
Health fellowship that
people can apply for,

00:11:21.840 --> 00:11:24.270
for your summer experiences.

00:11:24.270 --> 00:11:27.840
I see a few people who maybe
know what I'm talking about,

00:11:27.840 --> 00:11:30.720
but again, this is what gives
you experiential learning.

00:11:30.720 --> 00:11:32.340
You get paid too,

00:11:32.340 --> 00:11:34.800
to then be able to enter
the public health workforce.

00:11:34.800 --> 00:11:39.800
So to the point and question
about what is global

00:11:39.810 --> 00:11:41.940
and now local,

00:11:41.940 --> 00:11:46.470
I wanna just highlight even
more when we say local in the

00:11:46.470 --> 00:11:48.240
state of Connecticut,

00:11:48.240 --> 00:11:51.690
we think of the state as
local if you're first thinking

00:11:51.690 --> 00:11:53.356
about that. But in the
state of of Connecticut,

00:11:53.356 --> 00:11:57.900
we have 61 health
departments and districts.

00:11:57.900 --> 00:12:00.420
The city of New Haven
has a health department.

00:12:00.420 --> 00:12:05.420
We as a state need to work
with our local partners because

00:12:05.430 --> 00:12:09.810
that's who's actually on the
ground in the community dealing

00:12:09.810 --> 00:12:13.170
with the health crisis as it's emerging.

00:12:13.170 --> 00:12:15.870
And so we really have to take
local to the really local

00:12:15.870 --> 00:12:19.020
level and it's really on us
as the state health department

00:12:19.020 --> 00:12:20.760
to be able to work well.

00:12:20.760 --> 00:12:23.670
It's been one of my priorities
to be able to work well with

00:12:23.670 --> 00:12:27.810
local health to be able to
execute on these concepts.

00:12:27.810 --> 00:12:32.810
And what I would say is that
as we've heard, what is global?

00:12:33.840 --> 00:12:36.090
Global is local.

00:12:36.090 --> 00:12:37.833
As an ID physician,

00:12:38.910 --> 00:12:43.770
we were sitting here at Yale
New Haven Hospital waiting to

00:12:43.770 --> 00:12:46.410
see where that first case was
gonna emerge in the state of

00:12:46.410 --> 00:12:50.160
Connecticut as we saw things
coming and then to New York and

00:12:50.160 --> 00:12:53.280
moving up that I 95 corridor
and where it was gonna happen.

00:12:53.280 --> 00:12:56.790
So I saw it from the perspective
of a practitioner and

00:12:56.790 --> 00:12:59.340
director of the Yale
ID fellowship program.

00:12:59.340 --> 00:13:00.930
And you know, now are,

00:13:00.930 --> 00:13:04.020
am at the state level and the
number one thing that I can

00:13:04.020 --> 00:13:06.690
say is preparedness.

00:13:06.690 --> 00:13:08.430
It's those preparedness plans,

00:13:08.430 --> 00:13:12.000
it's going through the exercise
and recognizing that any

00:13:12.000 --> 00:13:15.720
threat can emerge at any time
and you need to be prepared

00:13:15.720 --> 00:13:17.190
for whatever that might be.

00:13:17.190 --> 00:13:18.900
You have to have plans in place,

00:13:18.900 --> 00:13:22.950
they may need to adapt and
change depending on the

00:13:22.950 --> 00:13:24.120
circumstances,

00:13:24.120 --> 00:13:27.870
but without a plan it is
very hard to execute on that.

00:13:27.870 --> 00:13:30.810
And we've learned so
much from the pandemic,

00:13:30.810 --> 00:13:35.810
which we have put into place
in terms of our plans and

00:13:36.240 --> 00:13:38.130
preparedness as a state.

00:13:38.130 --> 00:13:39.540
But without that,

00:13:39.540 --> 00:13:42.540
you're already behind the eight
ball when something emerges.

00:13:45.902 --> 00:13:49.330
- So both of you lead
sort of the health efforts

00:13:50.610 --> 00:13:52.713
in diverse states.

00:13:54.000 --> 00:13:56.050
Connecticut is a little bit more compact.

00:13:57.090 --> 00:14:00.420
Washington has both just not
just diversity in terms of its

00:14:00.420 --> 00:14:05.420
population, but also
worldviews and geography.

00:14:05.985 --> 00:14:07.860
So from, you know, Spokane,

00:14:07.860 --> 00:14:12.553
et cetera in the east to
Fairy County, which is,

00:14:12.553 --> 00:14:16.980
it was tea party before there
was a tea party to Vashon

00:14:16.980 --> 00:14:19.563
Washington, which is the
peak of crunchy granola,

00:14:20.900 --> 00:14:21.990
former hippie, current formerly hippie,

00:14:21.990 --> 00:14:24.300
currently rich crowd.

00:14:24.300 --> 00:14:25.849
And yeah,

00:14:25.849 --> 00:14:26.682
- Great place to visit by the way.

00:14:26.682 --> 00:14:27.602
- Yeah, wonderful place.

00:14:27.602 --> 00:14:30.150
The whole state is
wonderful and Yakima County,

00:14:30.150 --> 00:14:32.340
which is partially
populated, but you know,

00:14:32.340 --> 00:14:33.790
reasonably pro-public health.

00:14:34.898 --> 00:14:37.050
So with that diversity,

00:14:37.050 --> 00:14:40.920
how do you engage the community because

00:14:40.920 --> 00:14:42.665
it's not one community.

00:14:42.665 --> 00:14:45.158
How do you work with community leaders?

00:14:45.158 --> 00:14:46.500
Is there a model to do that,

00:14:46.500 --> 00:14:51.450
especially when you can't be
the health commissioners for

00:14:51.450 --> 00:14:55.020
just the conservatives or just
the liberals while being in a

00:14:55.020 --> 00:14:57.930
democratic society where, you know,

00:14:57.930 --> 00:14:59.180
appointees are political?

00:15:00.600 --> 00:15:02.593
- Yeah, so first of all, I,

00:15:02.593 --> 00:15:06.720
I would be remiss if I did
not join my colleague here to

00:15:06.720 --> 00:15:09.060
remind everybody that
we have great internship

00:15:09.060 --> 00:15:10.593
opportunities as well.

00:15:11.580 --> 00:15:13.084
Great volunteer opportunities.

00:15:13.084 --> 00:15:14.760
We're a great place to work,
we're an incredible state.

00:15:14.760 --> 00:15:16.590
So please come to the state of Washington.

00:15:16.590 --> 00:15:19.410
I just wanna let people know
in this room and beyond,

00:15:19.410 --> 00:15:23.430
you know, please come and we
are absolutely willing to,

00:15:23.430 --> 00:15:27.030
to recruit and we're not
shameless in the process.

00:15:27.030 --> 00:15:29.640
I just wanted to make that quick plug.

00:15:29.640 --> 00:15:31.230
What I would say a a couple of things.

00:15:31.230 --> 00:15:32.850
So, you know,

00:15:32.850 --> 00:15:35.370
I came from a deeply different,

00:15:35.370 --> 00:15:38.760
maybe that's the better
way to to say it, context,

00:15:38.760 --> 00:15:40.350
right?

00:15:40.350 --> 00:15:42.810
The state of Texas is on
the political spectrum,

00:15:42.810 --> 00:15:44.518
more conservative.

00:15:44.518 --> 00:15:48.900
It's what we refer to in the
popular vernacular of a red,

00:15:48.900 --> 00:15:52.300
red state than you go to
Washington and you've got

00:15:53.250 --> 00:15:54.083
a blue state.

00:15:54.083 --> 00:15:55.590
So you got red and blue.

00:15:55.590 --> 00:15:57.990
What I find, and you know, Dr. Omer

00:15:57.990 --> 00:16:00.191
you and I have talked about this,

00:16:00.191 --> 00:16:03.690
is that you get that diversity everywhere.

00:16:03.690 --> 00:16:06.483
I'm sure you get that right here.

00:16:07.380 --> 00:16:10.590
Five minutes from this campus,

00:16:10.590 --> 00:16:13.833
you may get it right here in this room.

00:16:15.300 --> 00:16:16.150
Think about that.

00:16:17.359 --> 00:16:21.540
We assume that everybody has a
certain political perspective

00:16:21.540 --> 00:16:24.450
or persuasion, that is so inaccurate.

00:16:24.450 --> 00:16:27.933
And so when I came to Washington,
my first comment at my,

00:16:28.800 --> 00:16:30.900
one of my press briefings was to say,

00:16:30.900 --> 00:16:33.390
I am the state health secretary,

00:16:33.390 --> 00:16:35.820
not just for one side of Washington,

00:16:35.820 --> 00:16:38.684
which everybody knows
is the I five corridor.

00:16:38.684 --> 00:16:41.280
Everybody thinks of Seattle
and just north and just south,

00:16:41.280 --> 00:16:44.040
where the majority of our
population is in the state of

00:16:44.040 --> 00:16:44.943
Washington.

00:16:45.842 --> 00:16:48.480
But I am the state health
secretary for the entire state of

00:16:48.480 --> 00:16:49.841
Washington.

00:16:49.841 --> 00:16:52.290
And then that played out in the
midst of our vaccination and

00:16:52.290 --> 00:16:55.351
our covid response efforts that no matter,

00:16:55.351 --> 00:16:56.790
and our tagline was,
no matter who you are,

00:16:56.790 --> 00:17:01.110
what you look like, where you
live or what you believe in,

00:17:01.110 --> 00:17:05.130
you should have access to
and fill in the blanks.

00:17:05.130 --> 00:17:08.160
And that is what health does.

00:17:08.160 --> 00:17:12.420
It levels and evens out when
there are elected officials and

00:17:12.420 --> 00:17:15.840
policy makers who are having
the partisan discussions.

00:17:15.840 --> 00:17:20.280
We must rise above that no matter what.

00:17:20.280 --> 00:17:24.300
We have to be connecting
across the aisle because our

00:17:24.300 --> 00:17:28.827
communities are on both
sides of that aisle.

00:17:28.827 --> 00:17:32.910
And shame on us in public
health if we're unwilling

00:17:32.910 --> 00:17:33.870
to do that.

00:17:33.870 --> 00:17:35.520
And that's where the diversity,

00:17:35.520 --> 00:17:37.800
not just of the community comes in,

00:17:37.800 --> 00:17:40.743
but the diversity of us as
practitioners comes in as well.

00:17:43.006 --> 00:17:44.610
- So you are right that there
is intellectual diversity in

00:17:44.610 --> 00:17:46.203
this room.

00:17:46.203 --> 00:17:48.673
There are indeed 15 shades of woke here.

00:17:48.673 --> 00:17:49.506
And I'm just joking.

00:17:49.506 --> 00:17:50.339
There is true.

00:17:50.339 --> 00:17:52.350
I have found true
intellectual dis you know,

00:17:52.350 --> 00:17:53.700
our students here,

00:17:53.700 --> 00:17:56.280
I'm very proud of the
fact that the discussions

00:17:56.280 --> 00:17:57.120
have been substantive.

00:17:57.120 --> 00:18:00.480
There have been multiple
ways of looking at the world,

00:18:00.480 --> 00:18:01.313
et cetera.

00:18:01.313 --> 00:18:04.020
And so you're absolutely right
in that coming to the more

00:18:04.020 --> 00:18:05.835
operational issues.

00:18:05.835 --> 00:18:07.590
So when you are doing a response,

00:18:07.590 --> 00:18:09.780
but also more broadly, so,

00:18:09.780 --> 00:18:12.960
so the job of a health
commissioner or secretary of health

00:18:12.960 --> 00:18:17.460
is not just strategic, not just visionary,

00:18:17.460 --> 00:18:19.470
but also operational.

00:18:19.470 --> 00:18:23.790
So how do you maintain
that logistical resilience,

00:18:23.790 --> 00:18:27.630
both for the emergency
response but also the,

00:18:27.630 --> 00:18:32.630
the other things people are
going to need sort of screenings

00:18:33.210 --> 00:18:36.210
and referrals and all
sorts of other stuff.

00:18:36.210 --> 00:18:38.310
So how do you operationally manage that?

00:18:38.310 --> 00:18:39.663
So maybe Dr. Juthani.

00:18:40.980 --> 00:18:45.980
- So I came into the job as commissioner

00:18:46.500 --> 00:18:49.050
in the middle of the pandemic.

00:18:49.050 --> 00:18:54.050
And within a few months
of being in the job,

00:18:54.087 --> 00:18:55.773
omicron took off.

00:18:56.913 --> 00:19:01.913
So I came into the job
when we were running fast

00:19:04.359 --> 00:19:07.890
and had to figure out the
job and figure out how to do

00:19:07.890 --> 00:19:12.420
emergency response while at
the same time not completely

00:19:12.420 --> 00:19:17.040
dropping the ball on all the
other routine public health

00:19:17.040 --> 00:19:19.704
types of things that we need to do.

00:19:19.704 --> 00:19:20.730
And we see the consequences,

00:19:20.730 --> 00:19:24.150
quite frankly in our society
of the fact that it was

00:19:24.150 --> 00:19:28.230
difficult to manage social
distancing and children being

00:19:28.230 --> 00:19:29.063
outta school.

00:19:29.063 --> 00:19:32.010
And we see that impacts
of that on mental health,

00:19:32.010 --> 00:19:35.430
on gun violence, on so many other aspects.

00:19:35.430 --> 00:19:36.900
Maternal mortality,

00:19:36.900 --> 00:19:39.240
congenital syphilis rates in Connecticut.

00:19:39.240 --> 00:19:42.930
We had eight cases of congenital
syphilis in the last year

00:19:42.930 --> 00:19:45.660
when we had years of none, zero.

00:19:45.660 --> 00:19:49.500
That is because of a lack
of access and accessing of

00:19:49.500 --> 00:19:53.220
maternal health services for many women.

00:19:53.220 --> 00:19:54.780
And that's a travesty.

00:19:54.780 --> 00:19:56.070
We know how to deal with that.

00:19:56.070 --> 00:19:59.340
That's just regular old public
health doing what it does,

00:19:59.340 --> 00:20:04.110
what medical care when it's
running in its usual way in our

00:20:04.110 --> 00:20:06.352
state really is.

00:20:06.352 --> 00:20:08.010
So I think for me,

00:20:08.010 --> 00:20:12.090
we were sort of flying the
plane and building it as it was

00:20:12.090 --> 00:20:13.530
going, you know, for example,

00:20:13.530 --> 00:20:17.460
self-test kits were just coming
out and we were trying to

00:20:17.460 --> 00:20:21.090
buy them and respond to
the omicron wave as it

00:20:21.090 --> 00:20:22.740
was all happening.

00:20:22.740 --> 00:20:24.150
And so, you know,

00:20:24.150 --> 00:20:26.850
now everybody's got more kits
than they know what to do

00:20:26.850 --> 00:20:30.210
with, you know, and, but
that was different back then.

00:20:30.210 --> 00:20:35.210
Now we're trying to continue
to rebuild and maintain that

00:20:35.790 --> 00:20:40.590
infrastructure on so many of
the general public health basic

00:20:40.590 --> 00:20:43.320
principles that we need to have in place.

00:20:43.320 --> 00:20:46.380
And it's a different tempo in some ways,

00:20:46.380 --> 00:20:48.846
but every day day there's a new emergency,

00:20:48.846 --> 00:20:50.190
quite frankly that comes up.

00:20:50.190 --> 00:20:51.690
It's this different in scale.

00:20:51.690 --> 00:20:54.120
There may be a smaller group
of people that are affected by

00:20:54.120 --> 00:20:55.200
that given emergency.

00:20:55.200 --> 00:20:57.210
It's not maybe the entire state.

00:20:57.210 --> 00:20:59.043
And I think that I,

00:20:59.043 --> 00:21:00.840
I just wanna make one comment
about even though we are a

00:21:00.840 --> 00:21:04.200
blue state boy, do we have
diversity in this state?

00:21:04.200 --> 00:21:06.330
We have rural areas of this state.

00:21:06.330 --> 00:21:09.150
We have all the same
disparate voices that you see

00:21:09.150 --> 00:21:10.320
in a red state.

00:21:10.320 --> 00:21:14.130
And we have the same challenges
even though we are generally

00:21:14.130 --> 00:21:19.130
viewed as a pro-public health
type of state where people

00:21:19.230 --> 00:21:21.930
often think people are
of the same mindset.

00:21:21.930 --> 00:21:23.310
That is not the case.

00:21:23.310 --> 00:21:27.960
And you do have to have an open
mind and recognize health as

00:21:27.960 --> 00:21:31.470
a human right and being able
to provide that to everybody

00:21:31.470 --> 00:21:35.793
and not just those who are
the same mindset as you are.

00:21:39.699 --> 00:21:42.550
- So when you arrived
from Texas to Washington

00:21:44.006 --> 00:21:47.580
in terms of you arrived when
the vaccine became available

00:21:47.580 --> 00:21:49.260
sort of right around the time.

00:21:49.260 --> 00:21:51.006
- That same week.

00:21:51.006 --> 00:21:51.839
- The same week, yeah.

00:21:51.839 --> 00:21:53.393
- My wife was not happy
with that decision.

00:21:54.480 --> 00:21:57.480
- And then sort of Washington
has done fairly well

00:21:57.480 --> 00:22:00.150
in terms of equitable
distribution of vaccines,

00:22:00.150 --> 00:22:02.670
in terms of objective metrics, et cetera.

00:22:02.670 --> 00:22:05.970
Obviously it took some
effort and all of that.

00:22:05.970 --> 00:22:08.733
What are some of the approaches that,

00:22:09.877 --> 00:22:12.727
specific approaches that you
applied to make that happen?

00:22:14.655 --> 00:22:17.550
- Well that's, you know, it's a long,

00:22:17.550 --> 00:22:20.321
it's a short question, long answer.

00:22:20.321 --> 00:22:22.110
I'll try to keep it very brief.

00:22:22.110 --> 00:22:24.570
There were a number of things
that we did and I did arrive

00:22:24.570 --> 00:22:27.210
the week of vaccines in the state.

00:22:27.210 --> 00:22:28.043
Fortunately,

00:22:28.918 --> 00:22:33.780
I arrived in a department that
was supported by a governor.

00:22:33.780 --> 00:22:36.418
I have to be very, very clear.

00:22:36.418 --> 00:22:41.118
Governor Insley was a champion
for health and continues to

00:22:41.118 --> 00:22:43.680
be, many people know him in
environmental health and climate

00:22:43.680 --> 00:22:46.350
climate change and the
impact on climate, on health.

00:22:46.350 --> 00:22:51.133
But that translated and transferred
over to his reading and,

00:22:52.737 --> 00:22:55.708
and studying and,

00:22:55.708 --> 00:22:57.810
and commenting and being
all in when it came

00:22:57.810 --> 00:23:00.865
to Covid 19 response.

00:23:00.865 --> 00:23:03.300
And so, yes, the first case,

00:23:03.300 --> 00:23:05.190
first outbreak, first death.

00:23:05.190 --> 00:23:09.210
And yet you fast forward two
years later and or two plus

00:23:09.210 --> 00:23:14.210
years later and we still have
the six lowest per capita

00:23:14.370 --> 00:23:17.070
death rate in the nation.

00:23:17.070 --> 00:23:21.720
And that's quite amazing when
you think about all that.

00:23:21.720 --> 00:23:24.810
So the specific strategies, Dr. Omer

00:23:24.810 --> 00:23:28.290
were really very much that
it was all in for Washington,

00:23:28.290 --> 00:23:30.450
that we had to do everything we could.

00:23:30.450 --> 00:23:33.900
We did the work with our
local health jurisdictions.

00:23:33.900 --> 00:23:37.140
We have 29 officially recognized tribes.

00:23:37.140 --> 00:23:39.750
So we worked with our tribal leaders,

00:23:39.750 --> 00:23:41.250
we worked with our faith leaders.

00:23:41.250 --> 00:23:43.080
We were in communities.

00:23:43.080 --> 00:23:45.810
We actually for the first
time had a state asset called

00:23:45.810 --> 00:23:50.810
Caravan where we deployed
state units that went into

00:23:51.900 --> 00:23:53.850
communities that communities,

00:23:53.850 --> 00:23:58.850
700 of them asked us to come
in and help with vaccinations.

00:23:59.070 --> 00:24:03.690
We had collaboratives for equity,
so we had community voice.

00:24:03.690 --> 00:24:05.910
We also had a public-private partnership.

00:24:05.910 --> 00:24:09.147
So in the midst of this is
really hard for government,

00:24:09.147 --> 00:24:12.000
government oftentimes stays
on the public sector side,

00:24:12.000 --> 00:24:13.524
right?

00:24:13.524 --> 00:24:14.610
So we had our Starbucks, right?

00:24:14.610 --> 00:24:18.000
We had Amazon, we had
Costco, we had Microsoft,

00:24:18.000 --> 00:24:20.160
we had Gates Foundation, we had Google,

00:24:20.160 --> 00:24:21.690
I mean just Alaska Air.

00:24:21.690 --> 00:24:23.190
We had all of these,

00:24:23.190 --> 00:24:26.610
these folks who were saying,
what can we do to help?

00:24:26.610 --> 00:24:28.110
We brought them together.

00:24:28.110 --> 00:24:30.360
So when Starbucks said to us,

00:24:30.360 --> 00:24:34.350
the governor asked us, there's
a specific example we need,

00:24:34.350 --> 00:24:37.830
I wanna make sure you're gonna
stand up for mass vaccination

00:24:37.830 --> 00:24:40.260
sites in the next 48 hours.

00:24:40.260 --> 00:24:42.300
And we did it, okay.

00:24:42.300 --> 00:24:45.850
But we stood them up and we
reached out to our private

00:24:46.826 --> 00:24:49.202
sector partner who were
part of our collaborative.

00:24:49.202 --> 00:24:50.467
And Starbucks said,

00:24:50.467 --> 00:24:53.190
if we can get a latte in the
hands of consumers 35% more

00:24:53.190 --> 00:24:54.870
effectively and efficiently,

00:24:54.870 --> 00:24:57.900
we guarantee you we can make
your mass fact site faster and

00:24:57.900 --> 00:24:58.733
more efficient.

00:24:59.720 --> 00:25:01.050
And guess what they were right.

00:25:01.050 --> 00:25:04.560
But shame on us in public
sector for unwilling

00:25:04.560 --> 00:25:05.880
to take that offer.

00:25:05.880 --> 00:25:09.600
That's the kind of all in
Washington approach that we took

00:25:09.600 --> 00:25:14.600
to unturn overturn every
stone to be able to respond.

00:25:14.700 --> 00:25:16.400
And that's how we were successful.

00:25:17.437 --> 00:25:19.650
- I think that's a really
good example of that

00:25:19.650 --> 00:25:24.195
as long as Starbucks is
not putting down names

00:25:24.195 --> 00:25:26.312
of people on cups or vaccines.

00:25:26.312 --> 00:25:27.573
So I've been called Sad

00:25:27.573 --> 00:25:28.406
- Not gonna comment on that one.

00:25:28.406 --> 00:25:30.750
- Exactly, SOB or SAD et cetera.

00:25:30.750 --> 00:25:32.610
Sometimes that reflects
the state of mind after

00:25:32.610 --> 00:25:33.443
I look at that.

00:25:34.872 --> 00:25:36.118
But other than that, no,

00:25:36.118 --> 00:25:37.410
it's leveraging sort of,

00:25:37.410 --> 00:25:41.820
it's truly all hands on deck approach.

00:25:41.820 --> 00:25:44.580
So both of you are not
only public health leaders,

00:25:44.580 --> 00:25:49.580
but are employers of
folks that come out of

00:25:51.420 --> 00:25:53.940
the public health educational system,

00:25:53.940 --> 00:25:56.130
a lot of them from the
schools of public health and

00:25:56.130 --> 00:25:58.293
medicine, et cetera, and nursing.

00:25:59.220 --> 00:26:00.090
What do you think,

00:26:00.090 --> 00:26:05.090
if you had the choice of
redesigning the skillset that

00:26:06.367 --> 00:26:10.631
people come out with from these
programs that is relevant to

00:26:10.631 --> 00:26:14.550
not just a health department
for applied public health,

00:26:14.550 --> 00:26:17.490
what would be your
advice if you were given

00:26:17.490 --> 00:26:18.323
that opportunity?

00:26:18.323 --> 00:26:20.806
So maybe we can start with Dr. Juthani

00:26:20.806 --> 00:26:23.227
and if you might wanna
add something there and,

00:26:24.578 --> 00:26:27.132
and that's something for
everyone, both faculty and others.

00:26:27.132 --> 00:26:27.965
Like, we need to be connected.

00:26:27.965 --> 00:26:32.130
We need to be responsive to
the needs of health departments

00:26:32.130 --> 00:26:35.073
and the broader local
and state public health.

00:26:36.600 --> 00:26:39.690
- So I think there's
different types of roles

00:26:39.690 --> 00:26:42.390
in local health departments
at the state level.

00:26:42.390 --> 00:26:43.680
So for example,

00:26:43.680 --> 00:26:46.440
epidemiologists that deal with data,

00:26:46.440 --> 00:26:50.670
you maybe get very good training
at learning how to analyze

00:26:50.670 --> 00:26:53.880
data, how to gather data
and be able to present it.

00:26:53.880 --> 00:26:58.020
I think some of the other
skills that are really useful in

00:26:58.020 --> 00:27:01.590
health departments relate
to project management,

00:27:01.590 --> 00:27:03.930
being able to provide deliverables,

00:27:03.930 --> 00:27:07.680
be able to present data more publicly,

00:27:07.680 --> 00:27:11.370
learning how to interface
with policy makers and

00:27:11.370 --> 00:27:12.390
legislators.

00:27:12.390 --> 00:27:14.340
And I know there are different
tracks in public health

00:27:14.340 --> 00:27:17.100
schools and some public health
schools are a little bit more

00:27:17.100 --> 00:27:17.933
research focused.

00:27:17.933 --> 00:27:21.810
Some public health schools are
a little bit more hands-on in

00:27:21.810 --> 00:27:25.530
terms of getting those skills
to be able to do jobs in

00:27:25.530 --> 00:27:28.050
public health and some schools
are sort of a mix of the two.

00:27:28.050 --> 00:27:29.850
And I think all public health
schools are trying to train

00:27:29.850 --> 00:27:32.190
people to do all of the above.

00:27:32.190 --> 00:27:36.690
But I think being able to get
some concrete skills in public

00:27:36.690 --> 00:27:41.220
speaking and presentation,
analysis of data and being able

00:27:41.220 --> 00:27:43.110
to communicate the value of the data

00:27:43.110 --> 00:27:44.850
that you are identifying

00:27:44.850 --> 00:27:47.730
project management to be able
to say you've been given a

00:27:47.730 --> 00:27:51.630
task and be able to see it
through and be able to actually

00:27:51.630 --> 00:27:54.450
deal with the nitty gritty on
a lot of these things are some

00:27:54.450 --> 00:27:55.650
very technical skills.

00:27:55.650 --> 00:27:58.230
I think some of the other
things that we see in our local

00:27:58.230 --> 00:28:00.300
health departments, for example,

00:28:00.300 --> 00:28:05.250
we in our in Connecticut local
health departments do a lot

00:28:05.250 --> 00:28:07.140
of the environmental health safety.

00:28:07.140 --> 00:28:11.040
So whether this is on septic
systems or sewer systems,

00:28:11.040 --> 00:28:13.710
whether you're doing
food protection safety,

00:28:13.710 --> 00:28:18.060
which is another huge thing,
you need scientific training,

00:28:18.060 --> 00:28:21.210
you need to have done science
classes to actually be able to

