00:40:46.740 --> 00:40:47.573
Global Health.

00:40:47.573 --> 00:40:50.490
We don't define global health
in the context of geography.

00:40:50.490 --> 00:40:54.940
We, our find core value is health equity,

00:40:56.340 --> 00:41:01.340
that all lives are created
equal and population's economic

00:41:02.700 --> 00:41:06.540
status doesn't have to
dictate their health destiny.

00:41:06.540 --> 00:41:08.670
And so therefore when you look
at it from that perspective,

00:41:08.670 --> 00:41:10.563
it's not about here and there.

00:41:11.490 --> 00:41:14.220
It's not colonial or post colonial,

00:41:14.220 --> 00:41:15.450
it's an orientation.

00:41:15.450 --> 00:41:19.290
It's about the shared humanity
and the shared journey that

00:41:19.290 --> 00:41:20.697
we all have.

00:41:20.697 --> 00:41:21.990
And that when you start
with that position,

00:41:21.990 --> 00:41:24.960
you arrive at these kinds of
things where you say that all

00:41:24.960 --> 00:41:26.790
of us have to learn for example,

00:41:26.790 --> 00:41:30.373
a community healthcare
worker program is a,

00:41:30.373 --> 00:41:34.890
a very essential public health
innovation that for once has

00:41:34.890 --> 00:41:37.440
some resources behind it and
we need to get that right.

00:41:37.440 --> 00:41:41.430
But one way we will get this
right is by learning from our,

00:41:41.430 --> 00:41:42.263
you know,

00:41:42.263 --> 00:41:45.150
partners around the world
who have done this reasonably

00:41:45.150 --> 00:41:46.320
well, not everywhere,

00:41:46.320 --> 00:41:48.840
but in more than enough places
that they're sort of valuable

00:41:48.840 --> 00:41:49.710
lessons there.

00:41:49.710 --> 00:41:51.693
I'll come to you for the question.

00:41:53.298 --> 00:41:54.150
- And as you're going to the next question

00:41:54.150 --> 00:41:55.564
I would just want to

00:41:55.564 --> 00:41:58.410
add with, when we were vetting
our transformational plan,

00:41:58.410 --> 00:42:01.260
we realized that there was
obviously resonance of what was

00:42:01.260 --> 00:42:03.486
happening in some of the larger areas,

00:42:03.486 --> 00:42:05.400
but when we went to some of
our rural areas, they said,

00:42:05.400 --> 00:42:08.280
you know, immigrant
populations and populations,

00:42:08.280 --> 00:42:10.230
agricultural farm workers or,

00:42:10.230 --> 00:42:12.600
or other aspects or what's
happening with our folks who are

00:42:12.600 --> 00:42:16.770
going overseas or back there
is resonance there as well with

00:42:16.770 --> 00:42:18.120
global and domestic health.

00:42:20.220 --> 00:42:21.780
- [Larry] Thank you.

00:42:21.780 --> 00:42:24.783
I'm Larry Vilano from
the Child study Center,

00:42:25.695 --> 00:42:26.880
psychology and psychiatry
next door at the School of

00:42:26.880 --> 00:42:28.683
Medicine professor there.

00:42:30.030 --> 00:42:32.940
So I wanted to ask you
all what your thoughts

00:42:32.940 --> 00:42:33.773
are about this.

00:42:33.773 --> 00:42:37.560
We all are under pressure to
kind of respond to the loudest

00:42:37.560 --> 00:42:39.810
voices, you know,

00:42:39.810 --> 00:42:44.810
the loudest student in our
class or the loudest patient.

00:42:46.800 --> 00:42:50.430
And so we all know that if
we do that we miss out on the

00:42:50.430 --> 00:42:52.650
quiet voices.

00:42:52.650 --> 00:42:53.483
And how do you,

00:42:54.397 --> 00:42:56.820
how do you think about that
and how do you defend against,

00:42:56.820 --> 00:42:58.301
you know,

00:42:58.301 --> 00:43:01.301
being worried about the loud
voices who have a lot of power,

00:43:02.430 --> 00:43:04.710
or at least they think they do and,

00:43:04.710 --> 00:43:07.320
and the quiet voices who feel
they don't have the power,

00:43:07.320 --> 00:43:08.599
you know,

00:43:08.599 --> 00:43:10.950
how do you do that and how
do you decide where to put

00:43:10.950 --> 00:43:15.950
the resources and efforts
and you know, not miss out?

00:43:16.320 --> 00:43:17.153
Thank you.

00:43:19.680 --> 00:43:23.910
- So I think some people are
very good at advocating for

00:43:23.910 --> 00:43:26.160
themselves and their issues.

00:43:26.160 --> 00:43:31.160
Some groups are good at doing
that and sometimes they're

00:43:32.160 --> 00:43:34.113
good at doing that for the quiet.

00:43:35.070 --> 00:43:40.070
And I think the strength
as a population is in

00:43:42.330 --> 00:43:47.330
community organizing and
creating a synergy of people who

00:43:48.240 --> 00:43:51.570
can advocate for a given voice
and a given topic so that

00:43:51.570 --> 00:43:54.540
your loud voice does become louder.

00:43:54.540 --> 00:43:56.940
Now that doesn't answer your question on,

00:43:56.940 --> 00:43:58.710
so what do you do as
somebody who's sitting

00:43:58.710 --> 00:43:59.880
on the other side?

00:43:59.880 --> 00:44:03.930
I guess I raised that because
I do think what I see from

00:44:03.930 --> 00:44:08.930
where I am now is that it is
true that the people who are

00:44:09.180 --> 00:44:12.360
able to organize and are able
to get their message out and

00:44:12.360 --> 00:44:17.100
are able to come together, do
get their voice heard more.

00:44:17.100 --> 00:44:20.220
But there are many groups that
have been successful at doing

00:44:20.220 --> 00:44:24.210
that for often voices that are not heard.

00:44:24.210 --> 00:44:27.150
So that's a credit to those
people and those community

00:44:27.150 --> 00:44:29.463
members who've found a way.

00:44:30.450 --> 00:44:33.044
Let's take gun violence as an example.

00:44:33.044 --> 00:44:34.200
You know, we have groups
right here in New Haven,

00:44:34.200 --> 00:44:36.300
right in Hartford that have organized,

00:44:36.300 --> 00:44:37.590
that have put themselves together,

00:44:37.590 --> 00:44:39.750
that are representing the grassroots,

00:44:39.750 --> 00:44:41.610
whether you talk about HIV care, you know,

00:44:41.610 --> 00:44:44.220
there's so many different
topics where you can get

00:44:44.220 --> 00:44:46.770
people who have shown that
they've been able to make their

00:44:46.770 --> 00:44:48.930
voice more heard, I say on,

00:44:48.930 --> 00:44:51.600
on the side of somebody
who's in state government.

00:44:51.600 --> 00:44:55.980
Now I think when we try
to address these topics

00:44:55.980 --> 00:44:57.180
that we recognize,

00:44:57.180 --> 00:44:59.880
'cause our job is really to
recognize what are the public

00:44:59.880 --> 00:45:04.170
health problems that are
really affecting our state.

00:45:04.170 --> 00:45:05.220
And when we do that,

00:45:05.220 --> 00:45:08.250
to have an open mind on
who should be at the table,

00:45:08.250 --> 00:45:10.950
who should be reflecting their points,

00:45:10.950 --> 00:45:14.640
and to be open in the way
that we bring those people

00:45:14.640 --> 00:45:15.870
to the table.

00:45:15.870 --> 00:45:20.310
And I think that I see that
happening in state government,

00:45:20.310 --> 00:45:24.030
but I also would say that
people who get the ear of their

00:45:24.030 --> 00:45:26.880
state legislature, their legislator,

00:45:26.880 --> 00:45:29.790
so for anybody who has an issue, you know,

00:45:29.790 --> 00:45:33.930
talking to people who have
that seat at the table already

00:45:33.930 --> 00:45:35.850
helps advance that point.

00:45:35.850 --> 00:45:38.040
A lot of people don't
feel empowered to do that.

00:45:38.040 --> 00:45:42.690
Every single one of us has
the right to contact a state

00:45:42.690 --> 00:45:45.180
representative, a state senator and vote.

00:45:45.180 --> 00:45:46.260
Quite frankly,

00:45:46.260 --> 00:45:50.520
these are the ways that
we can exercise our agency

00:45:50.520 --> 00:45:51.353
on these things.

00:45:51.353 --> 00:45:53.100
But I do think it's on us to,

00:45:53.100 --> 00:45:57.150
and what I try to do is bring
all those voices to the table

00:45:57.150 --> 00:46:00.513
anytime you're trying to
address those big problems.

00:46:01.650 --> 00:46:03.990
- I would just add intentionality.

00:46:03.990 --> 00:46:07.470
We have to really be intentional
in the process and Dr. Omer

00:46:07.470 --> 00:46:08.340
is an expert in this.

00:46:08.340 --> 00:46:09.660
I mean, this is an area where,

00:46:09.660 --> 00:46:11.257
you know,

00:46:11.257 --> 00:46:12.930
he has spent a lot of time
with both researching but also

00:46:12.930 --> 00:46:15.270
advocating and articulating this publicly.

00:46:15.270 --> 00:46:18.150
And what I would say is that
one thing that will remind

00:46:18.150 --> 00:46:21.540
ourselves is that we do hear
from the extremes on both,

00:46:21.540 --> 00:46:24.630
both ends and the vast
majority of Americans,

00:46:24.630 --> 00:46:28.500
the vast majority of Americans,
I believe are in the middle.

00:46:28.500 --> 00:46:30.450
They're truly in the middle.

00:46:30.450 --> 00:46:32.730
They're worried about
their kids going to school,

00:46:32.730 --> 00:46:34.800
they're worried about food on the table,

00:46:34.800 --> 00:46:36.630
they're worried about how their job,

00:46:36.630 --> 00:46:39.180
they're worried about the
stress of everyday life,

00:46:39.180 --> 00:46:41.400
they're worried about tomorrow.

00:46:41.400 --> 00:46:43.050
That's the vast majority.

00:46:43.050 --> 00:46:45.270
And yet the loudest voices get the,

00:46:45.270 --> 00:46:49.800
the airplay and that sucks
the oxygen out of the room.

00:46:49.800 --> 00:46:52.290
And what the biggest challenge
for us is to be intentional.

00:46:52.290 --> 00:46:55.560
So we can also speak to both
sides as well as understand,

00:46:55.560 --> 00:46:57.720
recognize what's happening in the middle.

00:46:57.720 --> 00:46:58.950
- So although not intentional,

00:46:58.950 --> 00:47:02.760
but we find ourselves as
at this last conversation,

00:47:02.760 --> 00:47:04.140
global health conversation series,

00:47:04.140 --> 00:47:09.140
event of the academic year with
an all minority panel and in

00:47:10.950 --> 00:47:14.220
fact for the first time
an all South Asian panel.

00:47:14.220 --> 00:47:15.870
And so I think it will be,
I'll take this over too.

00:47:15.870 --> 00:47:17.073
- Woohoo.
- Exactly.

00:47:18.687 --> 00:47:21.362
And you won't find any
of us calling that thing

00:47:21.362 --> 00:47:23.370
Chai tea latte.

00:47:23.370 --> 00:47:24.423
It's chai.

00:47:25.360 --> 00:47:27.120
And so.

00:47:27.120 --> 00:47:28.350
- Does everybody even understand,

00:47:28.350 --> 00:47:31.080
you might wanna explain that
one a little bit more, yeah.

00:47:31.080 --> 00:47:35.220
- So milk tea is chai, there's
no latte, there's no milk,

00:47:35.220 --> 00:47:36.510
you know, milk tea,

00:47:36.510 --> 00:47:40.938
it's chai and that's a
hill I'm willing to die on.

00:47:40.938 --> 00:47:44.340
(audience laughing)

00:47:44.340 --> 00:47:47.553
So coming back to that, so
coming back to the sort of,

00:47:49.660 --> 00:47:52.950
to the main point that
of course, you know,

00:47:52.950 --> 00:47:57.950
leadership is often about sort
of being the first in a room

00:47:58.320 --> 00:48:01.620
that hasn't seen someone who
looks like you or who has

00:48:01.620 --> 00:48:05.430
the same gender or sexual
orientation or the,

00:48:05.430 --> 00:48:09.600
the rural background or first
gen or the underrepresented

00:48:09.600 --> 00:48:12.120
minority or the immigrant background.

00:48:12.120 --> 00:48:14.580
And of course there are
fewer glass ceilings,

00:48:14.580 --> 00:48:16.329
but there are ceilings

00:48:16.329 --> 00:48:20.020
made of jello still there that
they put up resistance and so

00:48:21.240 --> 00:48:24.780
therefore they're often more
nuanced and harder to navigate.

00:48:24.780 --> 00:48:28.683
So both of you are trailblazers
from that aspects as well.

00:48:30.019 --> 00:48:32.280
And to put you on the spot a little bit,

00:48:32.280 --> 00:48:33.630
we get to do that in the academia.

00:48:33.630 --> 00:48:35.163
That's why God created tenure.

00:48:36.366 --> 00:48:37.710
I'll start.

00:48:37.710 --> 00:48:39.243
- For you, but not for us.

00:48:42.352 --> 00:48:45.690
- So maybe you can start Dr. Shah

00:48:45.690 --> 00:48:47.880
and then Dr. Juthani will end it with your

00:48:47.880 --> 00:48:49.320
thoughts on this.

00:48:49.320 --> 00:48:51.960
Something that, you know,

00:48:51.960 --> 00:48:53.823
lessons from being trailblazers.

00:48:55.680 --> 00:48:57.076
- You know,

00:48:57.076 --> 00:48:58.920
I think the lesson that I
take away is that yes, we're,

00:48:58.920 --> 00:49:01.770
we're South Asian from
a background standpoint,

00:49:01.770 --> 00:49:04.860
but it's more than that.

00:49:04.860 --> 00:49:06.600
That is where we come from.

00:49:06.600 --> 00:49:10.560
That's maybe our value
helped instill values.

00:49:10.560 --> 00:49:14.130
It's where our families,
our ways of learning,

00:49:14.130 --> 00:49:17.227
you know, how our parents
raised us, our culture,

00:49:17.227 --> 00:49:20.763
our food so much in
incredible rich heritage.

00:49:21.690 --> 00:49:24.810
But ultimately when you're in
the work world and when you're

00:49:24.810 --> 00:49:28.380
in the professional world,
it's about competence.

00:49:28.380 --> 00:49:31.350
And we wanna make sure
that the people are good

00:49:31.350 --> 00:49:32.970
at what they do.

00:49:32.970 --> 00:49:36.810
And that is the ultimate number one thing.

00:49:36.810 --> 00:49:39.510
But we also recognize to
the earlier question about

00:49:39.510 --> 00:49:43.770
intentionality, when
we are also looking to,

00:49:43.770 --> 00:49:47.280
to our workforce or to the
next generation of leaders,

00:49:47.280 --> 00:49:48.765
like all of you,

00:49:48.765 --> 00:49:51.480
we have to be intentional in
making sure that we're also

00:49:51.480 --> 00:49:54.120
broad in our perspective and approach.

00:49:54.120 --> 00:49:56.280
So we also look like,

00:49:56.280 --> 00:50:00.780
and start to mirror the very
communities that we serve.

00:50:00.780 --> 00:50:03.780
And that has not always
happened in this country.

00:50:03.780 --> 00:50:07.530
And that's why it's gotta
be an equity-based approach.

00:50:07.530 --> 00:50:12.270
And truly that is where leaders
like us can bring that trail

00:50:12.270 --> 00:50:14.280
blazing, if that's what it's called.

00:50:14.280 --> 00:50:17.070
I really believe it's really
the way we should operate and

00:50:17.070 --> 00:50:17.973
do our work.

00:50:20.010 --> 00:50:24.390
- So I think many people
saw many South Asians at the

00:50:24.390 --> 00:50:29.390
forefront of the covid pandemic
on TV in your hospitals,

00:50:30.000 --> 00:50:30.833
everywhere.

00:50:30.833 --> 00:50:32.460
And you know, the,

00:50:32.460 --> 00:50:35.160
I see us as standing on
the shoulders of giants,

00:50:35.160 --> 00:50:39.120
our parents and others who
came before us that helped

00:50:39.120 --> 00:50:40.530
get us here.

00:50:40.530 --> 00:50:42.900
And I think in terms of being, you know,

00:50:42.900 --> 00:50:45.270
the first or in these roles,

00:50:45.270 --> 00:50:48.630
these new roles that
are more public facing,

00:50:48.630 --> 00:50:51.300
I view that as an honor, privilege,

00:50:51.300 --> 00:50:56.300
and obligation to be competent
and to do the job well and to

00:50:56.880 --> 00:51:00.723
showcase for others that if
I can do it, you can do it.

00:51:00.723 --> 00:51:03.300
That all of you who are
in the room, you know,

00:51:03.300 --> 00:51:06.630
you are students now you
are inspired by COVID,

00:51:06.630 --> 00:51:10.980
You were here because you wanted
to be a firefighter because

00:51:10.980 --> 00:51:14.130
you wanted to be at the
forefront of addressing

00:51:14.130 --> 00:51:15.390
these concerns.

00:51:15.390 --> 00:51:20.390
And so I see that as our
challenge and goal is to inspire

00:51:21.990 --> 00:51:25.410
and show others that you too
can do that you too can join

00:51:25.410 --> 00:51:28.470
this fight as we try to advocate
for what's most important

00:51:28.470 --> 00:51:31.713
in health in our society
and in our country.

00:51:33.690 --> 00:51:34.523
- Thank you.

00:51:35.419 --> 00:51:39.930
Really appreciate it and it's
a privilege to have you as

00:51:39.930 --> 00:51:43.230
guests for this conversation series.

00:51:43.230 --> 00:51:45.570
And thank you for coming.

00:51:45.570 --> 00:51:48.990
As you know that this is a
new era for public health.

00:51:48.990 --> 00:51:50.640
You all have to do, you know,

00:51:50.640 --> 00:51:52.830
especially the students and
early stage faculty have to do

00:51:52.830 --> 00:51:56.913
better than than us, you know, than,

00:51:56.913 --> 00:51:59.550
than the current cohort because
there's no other choice.

00:51:59.550 --> 00:52:02.392
Because the next generation
of challenges at the state,

00:52:02.392 --> 00:52:05.310
local and global levels are existential.

00:52:05.310 --> 00:52:08.430
So the impact of global climate change,

00:52:08.430 --> 00:52:12.930
the fact that we have had a
shrinkage in life expectancy in

00:52:12.930 --> 00:52:14.700
the richest country in the world.

00:52:14.700 --> 00:52:18.240
The fact that this city's map
of covid mortality looked like

00:52:18.240 --> 00:52:20.043
where brown and black people lived,

00:52:21.120 --> 00:52:23.430
that should be unacceptable.

00:52:23.430 --> 00:52:28.350
But we have the privilege of
having the tools and being at

00:52:28.350 --> 00:52:31.050
institutions and having
access to the knowledge and

00:52:31.050 --> 00:52:36.000
understanding to gear up for that,

00:52:36.000 --> 00:52:39.210
that struggle, that fight, if you will.

00:52:39.210 --> 00:52:40.740
So no pressure.

00:52:40.740 --> 00:52:41.573
Thank you.

00:52:42.917 --> 00:52:46.313
(audience clapping)

00:52:46.313 --> 00:52:49.480
(light ambient music)

